notes.model:
Transfer and transport situation (Section 2, pp. 2-3).
- Carriers (players): $N \subseteq \mathbb{N}$.
- Points: $P_f=\{1,2,\dots,n,n+1\}$. Point 1 is the start, $n+1$ is the final point and the rest are intermediate. Transfers happen only at $P=P_f\setminus\{n+1\}$.
- Carrier $i$ must move volume $D_i\in\mathbb{R}_{\ge0}$ from point 1 to point $n+1$. On leg $p\to p+1$ it has capacity $Q_i^p\in\mathbb{R}_{\ge0}$ and unit transport cost $c_i^p\in\mathbb{R}_{\ge0}$.
- Assumption: $D_i\le Q_i^p$ for all $i\in N,p\in P$, so each carrier can always carry its own volume.
- Decision variables: $x_{ij}^p\ge0$ is the volume transferred from carrier $i$ to carrier $j$ at point $p$, which $j$ then carries on leg $p\to p+1$. $x_{ii}^p$ is the volume $i$ keeps (an 'internal transfer'). Binary $z_{ij}^p$ is 1 when a positive volume goes from $i$ to $j$ at $p$.
- Costs: a variable transfer cost $s_{ij}^p$ per unit (handling) and a fixed transfer cost $t_{ij}^p$ (investment), including the internal ones $s_{ii}^p,t_{ii}^p\in\mathbb{R}_{\ge0}$.
- Notation: $\theta=(N,P,D,Q,c,s,t)$, and $\Theta$ is the set of all situations. Table 1 (p. 3) lists the notation.

Problem (1) (p. 2) is a MILP: $\min \sum_{p\in P}\sum_{i\in N}\Big(c_i^p\sum_{j\in N}x_{ji}^p+\sum_{j\in N}(s_{ji}^p x_{ji}^p+t_{ji}^p z_{ji}^p)\Big)$ (1a), subject to:
- (1b) $\sum_{j}x_{ji}^p\le Q_i^p$ for all $i,p$ (capacity);
- (1c) $\sum_j x_{ji}^p=\sum_j x_{ij}^{p+1}$ for all $i$ and $p\in P\setminus\{n\}$ (what $i$ carries on leg $p$ is redistributed at point $p+1$);
- (1d) $\sum_j x_{ij}^1=D_i$ for all $i$ (all volume is assigned at the start);
- (1e) $x_{ij}^p\le M z_{ij}^p$ with $M\ge\sum_{i\in N}D_i$;
- (1f) $x\ge0$;
- (1g) $z\in\{0,1\}$.

The coefficient of $x_{ji}^p$ is $c_i^p+s_{ji}^p$: the receiving carrier's leg cost plus the pair's handling cost. Observation 1 allows replacing (1c) by '$\le$' and (1d) by '$\ge$'. From then on, 'Problem (1)' means this relaxed version.

Late entry of a carrier (for example at point 2) is modelled as follows (p. 2): its transport costs and internal variable and fixed transfer costs are set to zero up to its point of entry, and its external variable and fixed transfer costs are set high enough that they are never used.

CTT game (Section 3, p. 4). This is a TU cost game $(N,C^\theta)$ with $C^\theta:2^N\to\mathbb{R}$ and $C^\theta(\emptyset)=0$. For $S\neq\emptyset$, $C^\theta(S)$ is the optimal value of Problem (2), which is Problem (1) restricted to $S$:
- (2a) the same objective summed over $i,j\in S$;
- (2b) $\sum_{j\in S}x_{ji}^p\le Q_i^p$;
- (2c) $\sum_{j\in S}x_{ji}^p\le\sum_{j\in S}x_{ij}^{p+1}$ for $p\in P\setminus\{n\}$;
- (2d) $\sum_{j\in S}x_{ij}^1\ge D_i$;
- (2e) $x_{ij}^p\le Mz_{ij}^p$;
- (2f) $x\in\mathbb{R}_+$;
- (2g) $z$ binary.
All of these hold for $i\in S$ (and $j\in S$).

Core (3): $\mathcal{C}(N,C^\theta)=\{u\in\mathbb{R}^N:\sum_{i\in N}u_i=C^\theta(N),\ \sum_{i\in S}u_i\le C^\theta(S)\ \forall S\subseteq N,S\neq\emptyset\}$.

Subclasses studied (Section 4, pp. 4-5):
- $\Theta_{NF}$: no fixed transfer costs, $t_{ij}^p=0$ for all $i,j,p$. Constraints (2e) and the variables $z$ are then redundant.
- $\Theta_{UTC}$ ('unlimited fixed transfer cost'): $t_{ij}^p> D_i\sum_{p\in P}(c_i^p+s_{ii}^p)+\sum_{p\in P}t_{ii}^p$ for all $i\neq j$ and all $p$. The right-hand side is the sender's stand-alone cost (note-writer's reading).
- $\Theta_{UCETC}$ ('unlimited capacity with equal fixed and variable transfer costs'): $Q_i^p>\sum_{i\in N}D_i$ for all $p$, and $t_{ij}^p=t_{i'j'}^p$, $s_{ij}^p=s_{i'j'}^p$ for all $i,i',j,j'\in N$ and $p\in P$. As written, this also covers the internal pairs $i=j$.

Implicit assumptions (note-writer's reading): a single homogeneous volume type; every carrier must ship all of its volume (the paper contrasts this with commodity flow games, where players ship only the profitable commodities); linear transport and handling costs plus one fixed charge per used pair and point; deterministic data; no transfers at the final point.

results.statement:
From Owen [19], the Owen set, i.e. the allocations given by optimal solutions of the dual of the grand-coalition problem, is a subset of the core of linear production games. The dual of the grand-coalition problem of the CTT game is (4): max Σ_{i∈N} D_i η_i − Σ_{i∈N} Σ_{p∈P} Q_i^p γ_i^p, subject to:
(4b) φ_j^{p−1} − φ_i^p − γ_i^p ≤ c_i^p + s_ji^p for all i, j ∈ N and p ∈ P\{1, n};
(4c) φ_j^{n−1} − γ_i^n ≤ c_i^n + s_ji^n for all i, j;
(4d) η_j − φ_i^1 − γ_i^1 ≤ c_i^1 + s_ji^1 for all i, j;
(4e) γ_i^p, φ_i^p, η_i ∈ R_+.
Any optimal solution yields the core element (D_i η_i − Σ_{p∈P} Q_i^p γ_i^p)_{i∈N} of (N, C^θ) with θ ∈ Θ_NF.
